we may have developed  certain habits  and at that time to have those mistakes  pointed out may work i think the second  condition and maybe i should say that to  me there are two conditions for  correcting people whether it's spoken or  written condition number one it's best  to correct people when they are already  at least intermediate and possibly even  advanced and not before  and second of all that only correct  people who  give you permission to correct them  don't volunteer to correct people  so why we should be not correct  beginners because when you're beginning  there are so many things you have to  learn there are so many things that are  unknown that the number of corrections  that can be made are going to have a  minimal impact on you because you're  struggling with so many different  aspects of the language you won't  remember the correction  it doesn't relate to anything in my  experience i feel that initially with  the language i have to sort of expose  myself self start noticing things  gradually get better at  doing certain things there are certain  aspects of the language for example in  the cases in russian or tenses  and verb endings in romance languages  that you can be corrected but there's no  way you're going to be able to reproduce  them correctly even after having been  corrected you just need that additional  exposure experience and gradually you  will improve and then you may find after  a while that there are certain tenses or  certain verb endings or certain case  cases that give you particular trouble  and at that point it may be worthwhile  to point out  um  a mistake it may or may not have an  impact  um  you know uh but  so some people say well you might  develop bad habits i don't think you  develop bad habits when you first start  out everything is such a mess  you're struggling to understand  struggling to read struggling to speak  you're not going to develop any bad  habits  at a later stage though  you may  certain things may not sort of auto  correct so they then will become bad  habits but at that time  there'll be relatively fewer of them and  therefore to have them pointed out  there's a better chance  that you might be able to remember it  and then incorporate this correct way of  speaking  in your language but i can remember i  mean  talking to people when i'm giving  english uh you know having english  conversations with our learners at link  via skype and i was with a german  gentleman and he kept on saying berlin  you know i live in berlin berlin  and i said now in english actually the  accent is on the second syllable so why  wouldn't you say berlin because even in  german the accent is on the second  syllable oh thank you very much and for  the rest of the conversation he kept on  saying berlin like it didn't change and  he already spoke quite well  uh you know i have a very good swedish  friend and he has the sort of make some  of the typical swedish mistakes of  someone who speaks very well like  instead of saying there are  many swedes in sweden he will say it is  many swedes in sweden because that's how  it's set in swedish and i pointed out he  continues to make the same mistake it's  like the german smith  i i have been living in canada since  many years instead of four many years  some of these things i think if you  point them out at the beginning i don't  think it has any impact  as i say with the chinese who  because in spoken chinese there's no  difference between he and she  they will say you know my husband she  you know my my sister he  uh you can point it out and it may or  may not  i think  part of it is i know that i find that  when things are pointed out to me  uh either i have the feeling that  there's so many things that i'm  struggling to  get a grasp on that has no impact and i  pay no attention  or i know that this is something now  that i'm fairly good that i should be  able to do that and i want to be able to  get that correct you know and say it  correctly so then if i really am  determined to improve and focus on it  and it's not i'm not swamped by all  these other problems in the language  then at that stage  then uh i think the correction uh can be  helpful so  that's my take on it i think in the  beginning you know at at least until the  person is a good intermediate uh you  know the main thing is if they want to  start using the language now to  encourage them to use the language the  more they speak the better they will  notice a lot of the mistakes themselves  they will notice where their problems  are and hopefully start to look for that  in the language when they're reading and  speaking and so don't interrupt them  with every little mistake that they make  and then  later on as they get better and now you  can hone in on a few limited areas where  they need to  try at least to  correct some of their uh  call it bad habits or mistakes  but always with the permission  because the main thing in language  communication is just that communication  so if i'm communicating even poorly and  i want to communicate and it's something  that's important to me that i want to  say and i want to understand what the  other person is saying  and and if someone then starts to  interrupt me with corrections while i'm  speaking then  the communication  is no longer the main objective it's not  been changed from a communication  exercise  to a  to a teaching  you know scenario so if i asked for it  fine but otherwise i prefer just to  communicate which makes us wonder in our  school system why we put so much effort  into correcting the kids there the main  thing is to get them to speak forget the  correction get them to listen read and  speak  and if they actually do develop any kind  of ability to communicate in the  language it's never too late to go in  and make corrections later on i know  there are lots of  learners who think they need to be  corrected they want to be corrected  if they don't get corrected they feel  they're not getting their money's worth  from the class or something i mean  that's all wrong  in my opinion  the the initiative and learning has to  be with a learner  and no one is going to correct you to  fluency no one is going to correct you  sufficiently to  to make a big difference if you aren't  the one seeking out the language looking  for how it's  you know  thoughts are expressed proper phrasing  proper patterns  uh trying to communicate  um some up somewhat aware of what your  problems are if you aren't doing that if  you aren't taking the initiative to do  that yourself no amount of correction is  going to make much difference so i think  the initiative is with with us the  learner to get more of the language in  him or her to speak  with the intention of just wanting to  communicate don't worry about  corrections and at a later stage  probably by then a lot of problems will  have resolved themselves and at that  time  if you ask for it it could be useful  for people to correct you so there you  have it bye for now
 

